# Copy to config/sortie/.env and fill in. Add `.env` to .gitignore — never commit it.
#   cp config/sortie/env.example config/sortie/.env

# ── GitHub ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Fine-grained personal access token, scoped to this repository only:
#   https://github.com/settings/personal-access-tokens/new
#
#   Repository access : Only select repositories → this repo
#   Permissions       : Issues        → Read and write   (poll, label, comment)
#                       Contents      → Read and write   (clone, push the branch)
#                       Pull requests → Read and write   (open the PR)
#                       Metadata      → Read-only        (added automatically)
#
# Contents: Read-only is enough if you only ever want the planning stage — the agent then
# cannot push even if it tries. Grant write when you want pull requests.
# Token starts with github_pat_. Expiry is mandatory; note when it lapses.
SORTIE_GITHUB_TOKEN=

# ── Repository ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Both are derived from the git remote when left unset. Set SORTIE_REPO (owner/repo) if
# there is no `origin`, or if the issues live somewhere other than where the code does.
# SORTIE_REPO=
# SORTIE_REPO_URL=

# ── Claude Code ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Plan and design shell out to the `claude` binary and inherit this environment,
# so the CLI's own auth applies. If you are signed in on this machine (`claude` → /login,
# Pro/Max subscription), leave ANTHROPIC_API_KEY unset and it will use that. Thinking is the
# deliverable for both of these stages, which is why they get the stronger model.
#
# Set it only if you want those two stages billed to the API instead:
#   https://console.anthropic.com/settings/keys
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# ── OpenCode ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Building and review shell out to the `opencode` binary and inherit this environment, so
# OpenCode's own auth applies. They run on `opencode/deepseek-v4-flash-free`, a free OpenCode
# Zen model that needs no key and no sign-in. `opencode models` lists what is available. The
# failure recovery for a missing model logs as "Model not found: ..." at debug level.
#
# Set a provider key only if you want the build and review stages billed to an API directly
# instead:
#   https://platform.deepseek.com
# DEEPSEEK_API_KEY=

# ── Autonomy (optional) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Each stage decides for itself whether the step after it is worth a human's time:
#
#   SORTIE_AUTO_APPROVE_PLANS   the planning stage may approve its own plan and go
#                               straight to building, when the ticket is small, has no
#                               open questions, and touches nothing on the always-ask list
#                               in prompts/plan.md
#   SORTIE_AUTO_SKIP_REVIEW     the build stage may skip the code review and hand the pull
#                               request straight to you, when the diff is small, verified,
#                               and does exactly what the plan said
#
# Both default to on. Set either to `off` to make that step always happen — or label a
# single issue `needs-human`, which closes both gates for that issue only. Either way the
# agent says on the issue which path it took and why.
# SORTIE_AUTO_APPROVE_PLANS=off
# SORTIE_AUTO_SKIP_REVIEW=off

# ── Retries (optional) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Each stage waits progressively longer after a failed run — double the last wait,
# 10s → 20s → 40s → … → 160s → 320s → … — up to a 30-minute cap, and gives the
# issue up after ten failed runs. Set in the workflow files; override per machine
# with SORTIE_AGENT_* if this host wants different numbers.
# SORTIE_AGENT_MAX_RETRY_BACKOFF_MS=1800000
# SORTIE_AGENT_MAX_SESSIONS=10

# ── Logging (optional) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# How loud the orchestrators are: debug (default), info, warn, error. The default is
# debug because opencode can mask a real failure ("Model not found: ...", rate limit,
# auth) and sortie only recovers the reason at debug level — without it a failed run
# collapses to an unhelpful `turn_failed`. Four processes polling once a minute is a
# steady trickle of INFO lines that says only "nothing to do"; `warn` keeps failures,
# retries and escalations and drops the rest. What the agents themselves print is not
# affected by this.
#
# Each stage also serves a dashboard — plan on :7678, build on :7679, review on :7680,
# design on :7681 — which is a better way to watch a run than reading the stream.
# SORTIE_LOG_LEVEL=warn

# ── Auto-update (optional) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The stages load their workflow once at startup, so a merge to the code they run is not
# live until they restart. With this on, `run` pulls the default branch every interval,
# fast-forwards only when the tree is clean, refreshes the installed copies (when this
# repo was installed from the template), and restarts the stages once no issue is mid-run.
#
# Off by default: pulling and then executing newly merged code on a working copy is not
# something to switch on for every installed repo without them asking.
# SORTIE_AUTO_UPDATE=on
# SORTIE_AUTO_UPDATE_INTERVAL=300

# ── Ports (optional) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Each stage runs its own HTTP status server. Change these if you run the orchestrator for
# two projects at once, or if something else holds the port.
# SORTIE_PLAN_PORT=7678
# SORTIE_BUILD_PORT=7679
# SORTIE_REVIEW_PORT=7680
# SORTIE_DESIGN_PORT=7681
